I'm Scott Jurek and I'm an  ultra-marathoner and today we're gonna  be talking about injury prevention I  love pushing myself to the edge and when  I'm injured I'm not able to train I'm  not able to race and so avoiding  injuries but then once they do occur  making sure that I recover is paramount  to my success there are a few things I  usually bring in my pack from a  first-aid standpoint there's not one set  first aid kit out there it's really  important that you start thinking about  how long am i out there how far away  from help am I and that's gonna be  dictating what type of first-aid  supplies you need one of the most common  injuries on the trailer blisters you  really want to start treating them early  on into a run or hike to prevent the  blisters you want to avoid the abrasion  I like to use on petroleum jelly which  just kind of helps keep the toes the  skin moving smoothly between the sock I  commonly will include some moleskin  or some of the fancier added blister  prevention tapes and cushions that are  out there just try to figure out you  know what kind of conditions are you  gonna be in and what's gonna be best for  your feet another common injury is the  ankle sprain  treating an ankle sprain initially  involves compression and compression can  come in different forms I commonly will  have a compression sock or sleeve that  it can put over taping can be pretty  complicated so the nice thing with an  ankle brace that has support around it  with straps will create the same type of  tension and support without the  complications of okay how do I tape this  ankle if you have a cold creek or a cold  lake by you getting the ankle in some  cold water to kind of bring down the  swelling can be really key to making the  comfort level a lot more manageable and  get you back to the trailhead or maybe  continue on if you notice that pain is  like a dull ache you're probably okay  you know pushing through that if it's  more of a searing that's probably not  the pain too much through  another common issue out on the trail is  dehydration when the signs of  dehydration start first and foremost you  want to start drinking water if you  haven't been incorporating salt or  electrolytes I would highly recommend  that you bring some of those with you we  lose a lot of electrolytes and sweat  finding that happy balance between  injury recovery and performance is  probably like the ultimate goal of any  athlete or anybody trying to push their  body a little bit harder and when you  find that balance you're gonna be able  to push your body further than you ever  thought you could  [Music]
 

